  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 358 AM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nTowards the end of the work week, deterministic guidance shows \nfalling 500mb heights just to our west and shortwaves coming off of \nthe Rockies from a trough over the Western Canadian coast into the \nPacific NW. A high at the surface over the northern Great Lakes and \ninto southern Ontario will result in southeasterly flow at the \nsurface bringing moisture from the Gulf into eastern KS. With \nsynoptic scale lift and moisture available at the surface we could \nsee some rain in our area Thursday night into Friday but \ndeterministic and ensemble guidance keeps most of the rain off to \nour west until Friday morning with about a 15-30% chance of rain \nThursday night in NW MO gradually increasing to a 50-60% chance \nFriday morning for NW MO and 30-50% chance of rain for the rest of \nthe CWA. It is unlikely that we'll see any thunderstorms with this \nfirst round of showers as instability and shear looks to be pretty \nweak.\n \nThe greater chance for rain moves into our area this weekend, \nespecially Saturday night into Sunday. Guidance shows 500mb heights \ncontinuing to fall as we move into more zonal flow and the closed \nlow off the east coast starts to lift further north. The surface \nhigh over the Great Lakes continues to bring southerly flow and \nmoisture from the Gulf into our area supporting rain chances through \nthe weekend. We could hear some rumbles of thunder with the rain on \nSaturday night with guidance showing weak instability on the western \nedge of our CWA but otherwise we shouldn't see any severe weather as \ndeterministic guidance keeps the greatest instability outside of the \nCWA. We could potentially see some warmer temperatures this weekend \nand the beginning of next week with a 70-80% chance of highs above \n85, especially south of I-70.",
